course: money
lesson: 25
title: Saving for holidays and big things
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Big things like a holiday, a boiler or Christmas cost more than a family has spare in any one month, so families save a bit every month until the pot is big enough.
explain: Picture Dad's plan for a £1,200 holiday: £100 a month for 12 months, and by next summer the money is there, with nothing to pay back afterwards.
explain: Saving first beats borrowing: put the holiday on a credit card and you are still paying for the beach at Christmas, plus interest.
explain: Families also make big things cheaper: camping instead of flying, booking early, a good used car instead of a new one, or a free bed at an auntie's.
explain: A rainy day fund, or emergency fund, is money kept for surprises, so when the washing machine breaks, nobody has to borrow.
fact: Saving £100 a month for a £1,200 holiday takes £1,200 ÷ £100 = 12 months.
fact: An emergency fund is money kept for unexpected costs; a £250 repair from a £500 pot leaves £250.
fact: "A rainy day" means a time of trouble, so a rainy day fund is money saved for bad surprises.
fact: Cheaper choices can be just as fun: camping at £500 instead of £2,000 abroad costs a quarter, because £2,000 ÷ 4 = £500.
fact: Saving a little every week adds up: £10 a week for 52 weeks is £520.
fact: A children's savings account grows with regular saving: £20 a month is £240 a year and £2,400 in 10 years, plus some interest.
fact: Booking early is often cheaper, because prices rise as dates get closer; a trip at £400 in January might cost £800 in July.
fact: Depreciation is when something loses value over time; a £20,000 new car may be worth £16,000 after one year, losing £4,000.
fact: Writing savings goals down helps a family choose what comes first; goals of £600, £1,500 and £400 add up to £2,500.
fact: Staying with family instead of a hotel saves money: 5 nights at £80 is £400 saved.
fact: Putting a £1,000 holiday on a credit card and paying £100 a month takes about 11 months, because interest is added on top.
exercise: The holiday costs £1,200 and Dad saves £100 a month. How many months will it take? | 12 months | 10 months | 120 months | 6 months
exercise: What is an emergency fund? | Money saved for unexpected costs, like a broken machine | Money the government gives in an emergency | Money kept for holidays and nothing else | Money you borrow when something goes wrong
exercise: Mum's emergency pot has £500. The washing machine fix costs £250. How much is left? | £250 | £750 | £500 | £200
exercise: The trip abroad costs £2,000 and camping costs £500. What fraction of the price is camping? | A quarter | A half | A third | A fifth
exercise: Gran saves £10 a week for 52 weeks in her Christmas club. How much does she have? | £520 | £52 | £510 | £620
exercise: Kai's parents put £20 a month into his savings account. How much is that in a year, before interest? | £240 | £200 | £120 | £2,400
exercise: After 10 years of £240 a year, how much has been put in, before interest? | £2,400 | £240 | £1,240 | £24,000
exercise: Why does Ivy's mum book the holiday in January? | Booking early is often cheaper, as prices rise near the dates | Holidays booked in January come with free flights | Travel companies are closed in July | Prices always go down as the dates get closer
exercise: A trip costs £800 in July and half that in January. What is the January price? | £400 | £200 | £600 | £1,600
exercise: A £20,000 new car is worth £16,000 after a year. How much value did it lose? | £4,000 | £16,000 | £6,000 | £36,000
exercise: What is depreciation? | When something loses value over time | When something gets more valuable every year | A tax on cars that is paid every January | The money saved by buying second-hand
exercise: Mum's fridge goals are holiday £600, boiler £1,500 and Christmas £400. What is the total? | £2,500 | £2,100 | £2,400 | £1,900
exercise: A hotel costs £80 a night for 5 nights. How much does staying at the aunt's save? | £400 | £85 | £320 | £480
exercise: Why does Dad not put the holiday on a credit card? | They'd pay interest and still be paying months later | Credit cards cannot be used for holidays abroad | The bank would give them the holiday for free | Credit cards are cheaper, so saving is pointless
exercise: A £1,000 holiday on a card is paid off at £100 a month. About how long does it take with interest? | About 11 months | Exactly one month | Exactly 10 months | About 100 months
test: Your cousin goes, "Saving's for boomers, just whack it on the card, innit." What would happen if the family did that? | They'd pay interest and still be paying for the holiday months later | The holiday would end up cheaper than saving first | The card company would pay for half the holiday | Nothing different, cards and savings work the same
test: Gran, from the Black Country, says, "I've put a tenner by every wik since January, bostin'!" ("Bostin'" means brilliant; "wik" means week.) If she started 30 weeks ago, how much has she saved? | £300 | £30 | £130 | £520
test: Dad says the holiday fund gets £100 a month. "Nah, let's do £150," says Mum. How many months now for £1,200? | 8 months | 12 months | 16 months | 10 months
test: What would happen if a family had no emergency fund when the boiler broke? | They might have to borrow and pay interest on top | The boiler would fix itself after a while | The council would always buy them a new one | Nothing, because boilers never cost much money
test: A Scottish dad says, "Camping in the Highlands, aye, it'll be dreich, but it's a quarter of the price of Spain." ("Dreich" means grey and rainy.) Spain is £2,000. What does the camping cost? | £500 | £400 | £1,500 | £250
test: Your mate says, "New cars are always the best deal, they keep their value." What's true? | New cars lose value fast, like £4,000 in the first year on a £20,000 car | New cars gain value every year, like a house | New cars and used cars always cost exactly the same | Used cars lose value faster, so new ones are cheaper
test: In a video game, you get a holiday instantly by pressing "buy" with no money. What would real life need? | Saving up first, or borrowing and paying it back with interest | Nothing, holidays are free if you press the right button | A holiday ticket from the government for every family | Only a passport, because flights cost nothing
test: Ivy's mum: "Book in January, half the price of July." If July is £900, what does January cost? | £450 | £400 | £900 | £1,800
test: Aunt in Wales says, "Come and stay, I'll give you a cwtch and a cuppa." ("Cwtch" is a Welsh word for a cuddle.) The hotel was 5 nights at £80, but they'd stay 4 nights. How much do they save? | £320 | £400 | £84 | £240
test: Your little sister says, "Kai's £20 a month is tiny, it'll never be anything." What's true after 10 years? | It's £2,400, plus some interest | It's only about £200 after 10 years | It's £20, because savings don't grow | It's a full £24,000 after 10 years
test: Mum's goals: holiday £600, boiler £1,500, Christmas £400. The boiler breaks first. Why might she save for it before the holiday? | A home needs a working boiler; a holiday can wait | Boilers are cheaper than holidays, so they come first | Holidays are not allowed until every boiler is brand new | The holiday pot always pays for the boiler automatically
exam: It is given that a household proposes to accumulate the sum of £1,200 by means of monthly deposits of £100. Determine the number of months required. | 12 | 10 | 120 | 6
exam: Ascertain the aggregate of the savings objectives enumerated as follows: holiday £600, boiler £1,500 and Christmas £400. | £2,500 | £2,100 | £2,400 | £1,900
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term "depreciation"? | The diminution in the value of an asset with the passage of time | The augmentation of the value of an asset with the passage of time | A levy imposed upon the owners of motor vehicles | The interest payable upon a loan for a motor vehicle
exam: Identify the principal advantage of accumulating savings prior to a major purchase, as opposed to borrowing upon a credit card. | The avoidance of interest and of continuing repayments after the purchase | The receipt of a discount from the issuer of the credit card | The guaranteed reduction of the price by one half | The exemption of the purchase from value added tax
